WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:03.870
In this lesson we're
going to create our base email system.

2
00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.805
It's not going to have any memory yet.

3
00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.274
We're just going to set it up
and get it working.

4
00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.543
All right. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:13.046
The first thing we're going to want to do
is import

6
00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:16.049
some environment variable.

7
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:17.350
After that,

8
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.420
we're going to start to set up our profile
for this email system.

9
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:24.758
The first part of this is just basic facts
about the user.

10
00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:28.061
Like their name
and some background about them.

11
00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.331
This should be a profile for the users

12
00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:34.734
whose email this assistant
will be attempting to respond to.

13
00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.203
So you can change this
to be whatever you want.

14
00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.206
Probably your name and your background.

15
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:42.709
After that,

16
00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.712
we're going to define
some prompt instructions.

17
00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:50.417
These are instructions that will get
formatted into a larger prompt down below.

18
00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:52.919
There's two parts of this.

19
00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:55.522
The first is triage rules.

20
00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:58.525
These are parts
that will go into the triage prompt.

21
00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.095
The triage prompt is what is responsible
for triaging

22
00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:05.098
the user's email
into a few different categories.

23
00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.934
There are three to be precise.

24
00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.904
Ignore, notify, and respond.

25
00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:14.174
And we can see here
that we have instruction for what

26
00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:18.044
type of emails should be classified
into each of these categories.

27
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.414
A second instruction

28
00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.417
set is instructions for the main agent.

29
00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:28.955
This main agent gets called
after we decide that we should respond

30
00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:30.590
to the email.

31
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.793
So this contains instructions
for what to do in that case

32
00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:37.797
for both the profile
and the prompt instructions,

33
00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:40.834
these will get formatted into larger
prompts down below.

34
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.971
The reason that we're pulling them out
is twofold. One,

35
00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:49.008
this makes it more modular
and easier to see and visualize.

36
00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:54.848
Two in the future, some of these will
actually be generated as part of memory.

37
00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:58.118
And so we want them to be separate
from the rest of the prompt,

38
00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:01.121
which we are not going to update
as part of memory.

39
00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.560
After that, let's define an example email.

40
00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:11.397
This is just to see what the schema of
the emails that we'll be working with are.

41
00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:17.165
There's four main parts:
a from email address, a to email address,

42
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.773
a subject for that email
and then the body.

43
00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:25.645
We'll use this to test out
some of the agent parts down below.

44
00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.614
You can change
this to be whatever you want.

45
00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:34.587
Let's
now define the first part of the agent.

46
00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.623
This will be the triage step.

47
00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.626
First, we're going to set up some imports.

48
00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:46.299
Next, we're going to choose the model
that we want to use for this triage step.

49
00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:50.103
We're going to use OpenAI's
GPT 4o-mini model.

50
00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:53.106
But you can use whatever one you want.

51
00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:56.709
After that,

52
00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:00.380
we'll define the schema
that we want this triage step to output.

53
00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:03.650
We'll use a pydantic base model here.

54
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.218
And it will have two fields.

55
00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.953
First, will be a reasoning field.

56
00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:11.391
This is where the LLM can just generate
some reasoning for what it decided to do.

57
00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:15.261
And then next, is the main part
the classification step.

58
00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.665
This should be one of ignore,
respond and notify.

59
00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.869
We're going to bind

60
00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:26.039
this type to the LLM
with with structured output.

61
00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:31.544
This will make it so that LLM router
always returns information in this schema.

62
00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.985
Now we're going to import some prompts
for the triage step.

63
00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:42.155
There's two that we're going to be working
with the system prompt,

64
00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:45.158
and the user prompt.

65
00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:49.028
Let's take a look at the system prompt.

66
00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:52.031
We can see here
that it's got a few different sections.

67
00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.499
First, is a role section.

68
00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:57.470
This is where some background about
who the AI should be is placed.

69
00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.974
Then we have the background
on the user themselves.

70
00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:02.709
Then we have some instructions.

71
00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:05.411
This defines
the three different categories

72
00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.314
of things
that we want to classify emails into.

73
00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:10.083
And then we have a rule section.

74
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.786
This is where we will format
a lot of our instructions into.

75
00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:17.223
And so you can see the here that there are
these little curly brackets.

76
00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:19.158
This is actually a prompt template.

77
00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:22.161
This means that there are parts
of this prompt that are not filled in.

78
00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:24.364
And we're going to do that
with different variables.

79
00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:25.732
And we'll see how to do that.

80
00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:28.968
And then finally we can see that
there's a section for few shot examples.

81
00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.443
If we look at the user
prompt we can see that it's much simpler.

82
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.581
We can see that basically just formats
the email into a pretty simple input.

83
00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.754
Let's test this out.

84
00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.757
We can see here that we call triage system
prompt dot format.

85
00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.794
This will format the prompt template
with a bunch of variables that we pass in.

86
00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:58.798
We use information in profile
such as the full name,

87
00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.602
the name, and the user profile background
to populate some things.

88
00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:06.105
Examples we set as none
because we don't have any of these yet.

89
00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:07.540
And then the triage_no,

90
00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.877
triage_notify, and triage_email parts
we take

91
00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:14.047
from the prompt instructions.

92
00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:16.316
We do the same with the user prompt,

93
00:05:16.316 --> 00:05:19.952
formatting it with information
from the example email that we have.

94
00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.726
With that
done, we can now call the LLM router,

95
00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:30.229
which is this LLM
with the router schema attached.

96
00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:34.667
We pass in two messages a system message
with a system prompt as content,

97
00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:37.937
and then a user message
with the user prompt as content.

98
00:05:39.939 --> 00:05:40.707
If we take a look

99
00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.710
at what result
is, we can see that there's two parts.

100
00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.345
There's a reasoning string,

101
00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.547
this is the logic of the LLM.

102
00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.716
And then there's
a classification of the LLM.

103
00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:52.719
In this case it's saying to respond.

104
00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:56.823
Now's a good time to pause
and try it out with some different emails

105
00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:59.826
or different instructions
and see what you got.

106
00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:04.397
With the logic of the triage,
step out of the way.

107
00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:07.133
We're now going to tackle the main agent.

108
00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.168
First, we're going to define some tools.

109
00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.571
So we're going to import the
tool wrapper from LangChain.

110
00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:16.275
The first tool we're going to define
is a tool for writing emails.

111
00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.611
This tool will take in three things.

112
00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.214
An email address to send the email to,

113
00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.217
a subject of the email,
and the content of the email.

114
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.487
Right here we just have a fake dummy tool.

115
00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:30.490
So we're not actually implement
in any logic in it.

116
00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:31.557
In the real world,

117
00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:36.162
you'll connect this to your Gmail API
or outlook API to actually send the email,

118
00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:39.632
but here we're just going to mock it out.

119
00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.369
The next tool is a schedule meeting tool.

120
00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:47.140
This is going to take in a list
of attendees, a subject, a duration,

121
00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:50.476
and a preferred day,
and it will schedule a calendar meeting.

122
00:06:51.978 --> 00:06:54.914
Again, this is just a mock tool for now.

123
00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:57.517
In the real world
you would connect this to real APIs.

124
00:07:00.153 --> 00:07:00.820
And the final

125
00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.590
tool we're going to define is a tool
that will check the calendar availability.

126
00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.892
So we'll take in a day

127
00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:08.961
and it will return the availability
for the user on that day.

128
00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:11.731
Again, we're just mocking this tool out.

129
00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:14.300
So we're always going to return
9 a.m., 2 p.m.

130
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.303
and 4 p.m..

131
00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:20.206
With our tools defined,

132
00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:23.776
we're now going to go ahead
and create the prompt for this agent.

133
00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:26.712
So this is actually going to be a function

134
00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:30.216
that takes in the state of the agent
and returns a list of messages.

135
00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.686
This list of messages
is going to consist of two parts.

136
00:07:33.686 --> 00:07:36.389
One will be the messages
already in the state.

137
00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:39.959
And then the second will be a system
message that we put at the start.

138
00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:44.397
What exactly is in this system message?

139
00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:47.834
If we print out the prompt,

140
00:07:47.834 --> 00:07:52.138
we can see that there is a little bit
about the role that the AI is taking on.

141
00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:56.275
There's then a section that lists out
the tools that it has access to.

142
00:07:56.676 --> 00:07:59.679
And then there's a section
for the custom instructions.

143
00:07:59.846 --> 00:08:02.815
And we're going to pull these
in from the prompt instructions

144
00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:05.818
that we defined above.

145
00:08:07.587 --> 00:08:10.723
Let's now build
the agent. We're going to use Create

146
00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:14.360
React agent which is an off the shelf
agent implementation.

147
00:08:18.531 --> 00:08:19.799
We're going to create this agent

148
00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:23.302
by calling Create
React agent and passing in three things.

149
00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:27.206
First is a string corresponding
to the model to use.

150
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:31.611
In this case we're going to use
OpenAI and the GPT 4o model.

151
00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:34.847
Next we're
going to pass in a list of tools.

152
00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:37.850
In this case
the three tools we have defined above.

153
00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:40.152
And then finally we're going
to pass in the prompt.

154
00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:43.823
In this case, the function for creating
the prompt that we have defined above.

155
00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:46.292
We can now try it out.

156
00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:48.561
So we're going to invoke it
with the list of messages.

157
00:08:48.561 --> 00:08:53.561
And in this case let's pass in something
like "what's my

158
00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:56.969
availability for

159
00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:00.706
Tuesday?"

160
00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.276
We'll get back a response.

161
00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:07.079
This response will have a list of messages
as part of the state.

162
00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:11.317
If we print out the last message
in this list of messages,

163
00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:12.585
we can see that it responds with:

164
00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:15.855
"You have the following available time
slots on Tuesday 9 a.m.,

165
00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:19.191
2 p.m., and 4 p.m." Great.

166
00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.629
If you
remember, these are the times that we hard

167
00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:26.632
coded our calendar tool
to say that we were available.

168
00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:31.571
Let's now put it all together
and create the overall agent.

169
00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:35.975
The first thing we're going to do
is define the state of the agent.

170
00:09:36.876 --> 00:09:39.345
Here, the state is going to consist
of two parts.

171
00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:41.013
First is an email input.

172
00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.482
This is what the user will pass in.

173
00:09:43.482 --> 00:09:46.485
This will contain
all the information about the email.

174
00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:49.422
Second will be a list of messages.

175
00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:52.425
This is where the agent will do its work.

176
00:09:54.093 --> 00:09:55.595
After defining the state,

177
00:09:55.595 --> 00:09:58.598
let's import some things that we'll use
to build the rest of the agent.

178
00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:02.635
We're going to first define

179
00:10:02.635 --> 00:10:05.638
a node for doing the triage step.

180
00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:08.574
So this is going to take
in the state of the agent

181
00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:10.743
and it's going to return a command.

182
00:10:10.743 --> 00:10:12.778
A command is going to say two things.

183
00:10:12.778 --> 00:10:15.281
And we'll see this later on.
But it will say two things.

184
00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:19.118
It will return an update to the state
and then it will say where to go.

185
00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:24.523
And so these two values here are possible
values of where to go after this

186
00:10:24.523 --> 00:10:25.424
triage node.

187
00:10:25.424 --> 00:10:29.128
We could either go to the response agent
or we could go to the end of the graph.

188
00:10:29.161 --> 00:10:32.698
This is going to depend
on the result of the triage step.

189
00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:35.668
So let's actually implement this logic.

190
00:10:37.903 --> 00:10:38.504
The first thing

191
00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:41.774
we're going to do is pull apart
the email and put into a few different

192
00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:44.877
things that we're going to pass
into the prompt down below.

193
00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:46.512
This is just done for convenience.

194
00:10:46.512 --> 00:10:49.515
There's no real logic here.

195
00:10:50.716 --> 00:10:53.119
Next, we're going to create the system
prompt.

196
00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:56.122
This is the same as above.

197
00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.559
After that
we're going to create the user prompt.

198
00:11:00.559 --> 00:11:03.462
This is using the author, to, and subject

199
00:11:03.462 --> 00:11:06.465
an email thread information
from the email input.

200
00:11:08.234 --> 00:11:11.437
We're then going to call the LLM router
that we have defined above.

201
00:11:11.537 --> 00:11:13.806
Make sure this is LLM router not LLM.

202
00:11:13.806 --> 00:11:17.843
This is the one with the output
that will have the classification schema.

203
00:11:19.745 --> 00:11:22.214
We're then going to handle the three
different types

204
00:11:22.214 --> 00:11:24.283
of classification that can occur.

205
00:11:24.283 --> 00:11:26.585
Respond, ignore and notify.

206
00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:28.154
If it's something

207
00:11:28.154 --> 00:11:31.157
else we're going to raise a value here
that's unexpected.

208
00:11:32.058 --> 00:11:34.593
And at the end
we're going to return this command.

209
00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:39.593
This basically tells the graph which node
to go to and which update to apply.

210
00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:44.103
So each of these branches
for each of these classification results

211
00:11:44.403 --> 00:11:46.405
should set a go-to value.

212
00:11:46.405 --> 00:11:49.408
And an update value.

213
00:11:50.543 --> 00:11:52.311
Let's take a look at respond.

214
00:11:52.311 --> 00:11:55.281
This is what happens
when we want to respond to the email.

215
00:11:55.281 --> 00:11:56.982
First
we're going to print out some nice stuff.

216
00:11:56.982 --> 00:11:59.752
This is just so that we can see
what's going on.

217
00:11:59.752 --> 00:12:02.354
Our go-to is going to be response agent.

218
00:12:02.354 --> 00:12:05.691
So we want to go to the response
agent afterwards.

219
00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:07.727
The update we're going to apply

220
00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:10.730
is we're going to add a new message
to this list of messages.

221
00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:12.164
It's going to be a user message.

222
00:12:12.164 --> 00:12:15.167
And it's going to have this content
respond to the email.

223
00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:17.837
And then it's going to paste
in the email input there.

224
00:12:20.806 --> 00:12:21.207
When the

225
00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:24.243
LLM calls ignore
we're first going to print out something.

226
00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:26.545
This is similarly just for logging.

227
00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:27.947
Our update is going to be none.

228
00:12:27.947 --> 00:12:30.316
So we're not going
to update the state at all.

229
00:12:30.316 --> 00:12:31.717
And then we're going to call go to.

230
00:12:31.717 --> 00:12:34.720
We're going to go to the end node.

231
00:12:35.554 --> 00:12:37.156
What if it's notify?

232
00:12:37.156 --> 00:12:38.224
Here for mocking.

233
00:12:38.224 --> 00:12:39.992
It's actually going to be pretty similar
to ignore.

234
00:12:39.992 --> 00:12:42.995
We're going to print something out
with a slightly different print statement.

235
00:12:43.496 --> 00:12:45.531
And then we're still going to apply
no update.

236
00:12:45.531 --> 00:12:47.800
And we're going to go
to the end of the graph.

237
00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:49.602
In real life
this would probably do something else.

238
00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:51.937
This would go to
some sort of notification system.

239
00:12:51.937 --> 00:12:53.939
So you might want to go
to a different node

240
00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:55.775
if you add that into the graph.

241
00:12:55.775 --> 00:12:57.076
But here we don't have that.

242
00:12:57.076 --> 00:12:59.645
So we're just going
to do a simple go to the end.

243
00:13:01.247 --> 00:13:03.315
And that completes our triage node.

244
00:13:03.315 --> 00:13:04.416
Pretty complex.

245
00:13:04.416 --> 00:13:07.586
But hopefully you understand.
At a high level it's basically

246
00:13:07.887 --> 00:13:10.890
formatting a prompt calling the LLM.

247
00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:14.393
And then based on the output deciding
which node to go to next.

248
00:13:17.396 --> 00:13:17.696
Let's

249
00:13:17.696 --> 00:13:20.699
put it all together
and define our email agent.

250
00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:23.536
So we're going to use state graph
which is an agent

251
00:13:23.536 --> 00:13:25.738
that operates on a particular state.

252
00:13:25.738 --> 00:13:28.741
The state that it's going to operate
is on the state that we defined above.

253
00:13:29.074 --> 00:13:32.077
So it has an email and a list of messages.

254
00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:33.679
We're going to add two nodes.

255
00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:36.148
The triage node which we defined above.

256
00:13:36.148 --> 00:13:38.717
And then the response agent
which is a sub agent.

257
00:13:38.717 --> 00:13:41.720
This is the react agent
that we first defined.

258
00:13:41.954 --> 00:13:44.723
We're going to add an edge
from start to the triage router.

259
00:13:44.723 --> 00:13:46.725
This is basically just saying

260
00:13:46.725 --> 00:13:50.563
that the first node we want to go into
after the start is this triage step.

261
00:13:51.096 --> 00:13:52.565
And then we're
going to compile this agent.

262
00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:56.702
Before using it,

263
00:13:56.702 --> 00:14:00.105
let's check out what it looks like.
So we can use this

264
00:14:00.105 --> 00:14:03.843
draw mermaid PNG method on the line

265
00:14:03.876 --> 00:14:07.246
graph object to get a nice mermaid
drawing of what it looks like.

266
00:14:07.646 --> 00:14:09.748
And we're going to pass this
x ray equals true.

267
00:14:09.748 --> 00:14:12.885
This is just allowing us to zoom in
to this response agent.

268
00:14:13.719 --> 00:14:15.588
Because this is a sub agent in itself.

269
00:14:15.588 --> 00:14:17.756
So we want to visualize it.

270
00:14:17.756 --> 00:14:20.860
So if we look at the graph we can see that
we first go to this triage step.

271
00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:24.697
From there either goes to end
or it goes into the response agent.

272
00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:27.466
This response agent is of course
an agent itself.

273
00:14:27.466 --> 00:14:31.904
It's got this nice little react
loop here of an LLM, calling something

274
00:14:31.904 --> 00:14:35.374
and then calling some tools
and then going back, or it can finish.

275
00:14:38.844 --> 00:14:40.779
Let's try it out on a few emails.

276
00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:43.115
So, first here's one
that looks like some spam.

277
00:14:43.115 --> 00:14:45.985
It's spamming some nice sales email.

278
00:14:45.985 --> 00:14:48.621
Hopefully this will be ignored
by the email

279
00:14:48.621 --> 00:14:51.624
agent.

280
00:14:52.124 --> 00:14:52.725
Let's call it

281
00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:56.495
with this email input and we can see that
it prints out this classification

282
00:14:56.495 --> 00:14:57.496
Ignore.

283
00:14:57.496 --> 00:15:00.099
So here we can tell
that it's successfully ignoring this

284
00:15:00.099 --> 00:15:03.102
email.

285
00:15:05.137 --> 00:15:06.939
Let's try out another example.

286
00:15:06.939 --> 00:15:09.942
This time one that expects a response.

287
00:15:12.745 --> 00:15:13.545
If we call it

288
00:15:13.545 --> 00:15:16.782
we can see that
we print out this classification respond.

289
00:15:16.815 --> 00:15:19.385
This means that we're getting a response.

290
00:15:19.385 --> 00:15:22.855
But what exactly is happening?
In order to see that,

291
00:15:23.088 --> 00:15:26.091
let's take a look at the list of messages.

292
00:15:26.425 --> 00:15:28.027
First, we can see the human message.

293
00:15:28.027 --> 00:15:32.598
This is the human message that the triage
node puts in to the list of messages.

294
00:15:33.499 --> 00:15:37.636
We can then see that
it calls the right email tool. In here

295
00:15:37.636 --> 00:15:40.773
it responds to Alice
thanking her for reaching out.

296
00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:42.641
We can then

297
00:15:42.641 --> 00:15:46.312
see that there's a tool message basically
just saying that we sent this message.

298
00:15:46.745 --> 00:15:50.683
And then we can see a final AI message
saying, I've responded to Alice's email.

299
00:15:50.716 --> 00:15:52.918
Blah blah blah.

300
00:15:52.918 --> 00:15:55.921
This is the complete email
agent in action.

301
00:15:55.921 --> 00:16:00.826
It first triages the email and then it
handles it accordingly if it's meant to.

302
00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:04.730
Now's a good time to pause
and try out some other emails.

303
00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:06.865
Try out some other instructions.

304
00:16:06.865 --> 00:16:09.969
Try out messing around with the system
prompt and things like that.

305
00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:14.807
This concludes building the email agent,
